# lattice

Your pages, your knowledge, your ship.

A personal knowledge platform that runs on your own Urbit ship. Write pages in markdown, gemtext, or HTML, or in Hoon so they compute their own content. Keep a private, tagged knowledge store that you and your AI agents share. Search all of it. Everything you publish is addressed urb://~ship/path and travels peer-to-peer between ships, with no DNS, no web server, and no host in the middle.

(You may well be reading this page through lattice right now.)

## How it works

lattice's ship side is a nexus inside the grubbery framework. Your pages and your knowledge live in grubbery's vault on your ship, and the client is served by the ship too.

* The ship: one desk, one command. |install ~ricsul-bilwyt %grubbery pulls the desk, boots the lattice nexus and binds /apps/lattice. Updates arrive on their own through kiln.
* The client: /apps/lattice to read, /apps/lattice/app to write. On a phone, add it to your home screen: it is a full PWA that works offline.
* The surfaces: an optional desktop app for Linux and macOS, a FUSE mount that makes your pages plain files, and eleven MCP tools your AI assistant uses as memory. One store behind all of them.

## What it does

* Pages, not just gemtext: markdown, gemtext, HTML, plain text, JS, CSS, or Hoon pages that compute their content, with commands, state, and dependencies on other pages.
* Knowledge store: a private, tagged note store with per-entry history and a trash. Browse it with folders and tag filters; drive it from the web app, HTTP, MCP, or the filesystem mount.
* Search: a term index over pages and knowledge, from the reader's omnibar, every hit badged with its scope.
* A real filesystem: lattice-fs mounts your pages as local files, so grep and cat run from RAM and your editor's backup files never touch the ship.
* Publish: every saved page is a signed value in the Urbit namespace, readable as urb://~you/that/path. Pages and folders can also go clearweb-public over plain HTTP. Share a folder and you've published a site.
* Editor: highlighting for every page kind, live preview, compile errors for Hoon pages, templates, folder-level share, move and delete, drag-and-drop upload of files or directories. A page named "My Page" keeps that name in the tree.
* Works offline: saves, deletes, moves and renames queue locally and replay in order when the ship returns; an overwritten concurrent edit is kept as a real page under conflicts/.
* Export and restore: every page and memory as one tar of plain files; restore puts them back with paths, tags and dates intact. The desktop app schedules backups to a folder on your machine.
* Sharing that scales: named groups of ships with per-path read and edit grants, per-file grants, a banlist that revokes on ban, and comments from readers with a moderation inbox.
* Follow and subscribe: follow ships, or subscribe to one file and get each edit as it happens over a live namespace subscription.
* Browse urb://: read what any ship publishes, bookmark it, fetch urb://~ship/manifest for a stranger's index, or copy a remote file onto your own ship.
* Light and dark, following your system.

## Memory for your AI agents

The knowledge store speaks MCP through eleven tools compiled into the ship: save, read, list, search, explore by tag, move, delete, restore, tag, untag, and list tags. Point your MCP client at https://your-ship/grubbery/mcp with a normal ship session cookie. Nothing runs beside the ship and nothing is registered; your assistant's memory lives on your ship.

## What it isn't

* Not a host. Bring your own ship.
* Not the HTTP web, except for what you choose to publish clearweb, which your ship serves itself.
* Not an app to install. The client is served by your ship; the desktop app and the filesystem mount are optional extras.

## Install

In your dojo:

```
|install ~ricsul-bilwyt %grubbery
```

Then log into your ship's web login with your +code and open /apps/lattice. The optional desktop app for Linux and macOS is on the releases page:

=> https://github.com/nisfeb/lattice/releases/latest  Releases
=> https://github.com/nisfeb/lattice  Source on GitHub
=> https://urbit.org/overview/running-urbit  New to Urbit? Boot a ship

